WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:07.049
Millions of Pokemon Go trainers around the
world are exploring their communities in a

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:08.260
quest to catch 'em all.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.940
The app doesn't have very much of an in-game
tutorial system, so we've compiled a list

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.760
of the most essential things you should know
before setting forth in search of a Dragonair

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.680
or Mewtwo.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.030
I choose you, Pikachu!

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:22.690
Want to be just like Ash and get Pikachu as
your starter Pokemon?

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:23.970
It takes a little bit of work.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.520
When Professor Willow presents you with a
Bulbasaur, Squirtle, and Charmander on your

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.600
map, simply walk away from them.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.149
Once you get out of range, the three critters
will appear again next to you.

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:36.050
Repeat the process, walking away from them
four times.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.100
The next time they reappear, a Pikachu will
also be there waiting for you.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.080
And the circle is complete.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.640
Sort your Pokemon

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.550
Tired of scrolling through your bag in search
of that Clefairy you know you caught yesterday?

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.710
Take advantage of the in-game sorting options.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.329
On the Pokemon view interface, tap the icon
at the bottom right of the screen.

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:57.780
You'll be given numerous sorting options to
choose from, including name, time caught,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.649
combat power, hit points, Pokedex number,
and other information.

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:02.649
Pokemon tracking

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:06.909
Pokemon that show up in the "nearby" list
won't come closer to you if you use an Incense

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:11.530
or Lure Module, since those are determined
randomly and don't require movement for them

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.530
to spawn.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.250
The number of footprints below a Pokemon in
the list indicates their distance, and each

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.439
footprint represents approximately 30 to 50
meters.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:22.610
You can tap on the list to open a grid and
use it to navigate.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.940
Tap on your target in the list, then start
walking.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.790
As you get closer, the targeted critter will
move up and to the left on the list.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:32.320
If you're walking in the wrong direction,
the Pokemon will move down and to the right

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.320
on the grid.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.290
When it reaches the top-left position on the
grid, it will be the closest Pokemon to you.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.740
Keep homing in and the footprints will drop
to two, then one, then none when you're right

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:42.850
next to it.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.400
Great throws

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.420
Catching Pokemon isn't as simple as just chucking
a Pokeball at an animal’s head.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.310
When you target a creature, holding your finger
down on the Pokeball will generate a shrinking

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.350
target ring.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.670
The ring colors indicate how difficult each
creature is to catch.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.280
Green is easy, yellow is average, orange is
difficult, and red is very hard.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.120
Toss your Pokeball through the colored circle
as it shrinks.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.650
Accuracy nets additional XP and a higher catch
chance.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.960
You can spin your Pokeball until it sparkles
right before you toss it and get a "Curveball"

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:12.960
XP bonus.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.590
Super Balls and Ultra Balls can make catching
Pokemon with orange and red targeting circles

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:17.590
easier.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:21.709
Pokemon can be bribed with "razz berries"
which also increase your chances… but only

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:23.400
for one throw.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.980
Fighting gym battles

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.659
Just like catching Pokemon, battling with
them is more complicated than it seems.

00:02:28.659 --> 00:02:32.840
Your creatures all have two attack moves:
a primary move and a "special" one that must

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.840
charge up.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.249
When fighting at a gym, tap your Pokemon to
use the primary move, and keep an eye on the

00:02:37.249 --> 00:02:39.629
special move bar located below your Pokemon's
name.

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:43.299
When the special meter is full, hold down
your finger on your Pokemon to unleash their

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:44.299
special move.

00:02:44.299 --> 00:02:47.620
Don't forget you can also swipe left or right
to dodge incoming attacks.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.319
Keep these tips in mind and your next fight
should be super-effective.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:52.989
Diversity is key

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:57.059
Before you go charging into an enemy Gym with
six wimpy Pidgeys, remember that Pokemon types

00:02:57.059 --> 00:03:00.340
are essentially a more complex version of
Rock, Paper, Scissors.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:04.599
Grass beats water, water beats fire, fire
beats grass, and so on, so you should build

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:08.349
a diverse team that's best suited to fight
the Pokemon defending the gym.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:11.760
When you initiate a gym battle, you'll be
shown your team of six at the bottom of your

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:12.760
screen.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.109
Tap any Pokemon to switch them out with another
from your collection, then start the battle

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:17.469
when you're ready.

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:18.900
Gym takeovers

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.230
If you win a battle at an enemy gym, you may
be surprised when the enemy team retains possession

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.230
of it.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.890
That's because gym ownership is based on overall
team prestige.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.629
You may have to battle a gym successfully
multiple times in a row to lower the gym's

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:31.859
prestige to zero.

00:03:31.859 --> 00:03:34.809
Bring friends along to make the takeover process
faster.

00:03:34.809 --> 00:03:38.760
Once it hits zero, the gym will become neutral
and you can install one of your own Pokemon

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.760
to defend it.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.069
Gym leaders are determined by which Pokemon
at that gym leads in Combat Points, or CP.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:47.609
For every gym you lead or support, you'll
get a daily package filled with Pokecoins

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:49.659
for use in the online shop.

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:51.150
Train your Pokemon

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.339
Gyms also have levels of their own, based
on their battle prestige.

00:03:54.339 --> 00:03:58.099
The higher level the gym is, the more people
on your team can put Pokemon in the gym.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:02.489
The more Pokemon there are in a gym, the harder
it is for challengers to initiate a takeover.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:06.369
Training your Pokemon is simple—just visit
a Gym that's already controlled by your team.

00:04:06.369 --> 00:04:09.799
Once there, you can train by battling against
your own team's Pokemon.

00:04:09.799 --> 00:04:13.370
Defeat all of them and your Gym's Prestige
will go up, making it more difficult to be

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:17.900
defeated by another team, and also opening
new slots for more defenders to be added.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.009
Smart evolutions

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:24.110
Hold on to your highest CP Pokemon, and transfer
lower-level duplicates to Professor Willow

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.550
in exchange for candy.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:28.729
Don't waste your candy evolving a Pokemon
if it has a low CP bar.

00:04:28.729 --> 00:04:32.270
While it'll still get a relative boost in
power, it will be in the exact same position

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:36.949
on the CP arc in its evolved form, and cost
you more candy and Stardust to power up.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:41.940
Aim to evolve Pokemon that are already strong
in CP, or power them up first before evolving.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.699
If a Pokemon's CP arc is completely full,
that means you cannot power them up any further

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:48.160
due to your trainer's level.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.319
Level up your trainer first, and then that
Pokemon will have space to be powered up again.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:54.490
Battery saver mode and battery packs

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.360
Here's a tip for trainers in the real world,
not the digital one.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.509
The game and the GPS tracking it can drain
your phone's battery life quickly.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.879
Additionally, you can leave the app open to
get notifications of nearby Pokemon.

00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:09.340
To get the most game time out of your device,
close your other apps and turn on your phone's

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.340
"Battery Saver" option.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.699
You should also invest in a couple of portable
power banks to charge your phone.

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:17.590
Only buy ones that pack at least 10,000 milliamp
hours of charge, and you should be able to

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.419
stay in business catching Pokemon for several
hours straight.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.480
Just don't blame us if you use up all your
data playing Pokemon Go.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.520
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.410
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:31.039
And leave us a comment to let us know what
Pokemon Go tips you think are super effective...

